# Results and the conditions they hold under (experiment-repo, as of commit after 077ae91)

Every number below is read from a file in `results/` (file named in brackets). Primary design: grouped 10-fold by model family (21 / 247-column MTEB multi / 79 HELM
models), fixed 30 % target columns, greedy A-optimal selector, FA predictor unless said otherwise; intervals are family-clustered bootstrap (B=2000, seed 0).
Metrics are RMSE on the original standardised scale. Transform (identity or logit) was chosen on training folds by held-out likelihood:
helm_mmlu logit, mteb_eng identity, mteb_multi logit [`transform.csv`]. Departures from the Brief are in `code/DEVIATIONS.md`.

## H2: greedy A vs random, grid average over k=1..min(10,N/2) [`gap.csv`, `robustness.csv`]
* Gap in RMSE (greedy A minus random), CI: helm_mmlu -0.044 [-0.065, -0.026] (-7.6 % of random); mteb_eng -0.038 [-0.058, -0.013] (-6.1 %);
  mteb_multi -0.076 [-0.085, -0.066] (-11.3 %). The CI excludes 0 on all three; the pre-registered margin (gap >= 10 % of random RMSE) is met only on mteb_multi.
* Same sign on random and time-forward splits (gap.csv, exploratory rows). Under the non-chosen transform on mteb_eng (logit) the gap shrinks to -2.6 %
  [`robustness.csv`]; so the result depends on the transform choice there.
* Adaptive (per-model, local-A) selection in the identity space [`adaptive.csv`]: against greedy A the RMSE gain is nil on mteb_eng (FA +0.0003, CI [-0.010, 0.016]),
  helm_mmlu (FA -0.005, CI [-0.010, 0.003]) and small on mteb_multi (FA -0.014 [-0.030, -0.005]; kNN -0.008 [-0.014, -0.003]). Greedy A is within about
  0.01 RMSE of the adaptive rule everywhere.

## H1 and k* [`h1.csv`, `kstar.csv`, `closure.csv`]
* Headline k* (90 % closure to the leave-one-benchmark-out reference, grouped): helm_mmlu 1 (CI 1-2, "dominated by general factor"); mteb_eng 9 (CI 3-31, "above window");
  mteb_multi 30 = grid maximum (CI 8-31, "above window"). Time-forward split: mteb_eng 30 (31.6 % of bootstrap draws censored), mteb_multi 15.
* k* is fragile on the two MTEB matrices: intervals are wide, depend on the threshold, the predictor (mteb_eng FA-only 20 vs headline 9) and, on mteb_multi,
  on the transform (identity gives 15, logit is censored beyond the grid for FA). Report k* with its CI and censoring share, never as a point value.
* Per-benchmark skill at k=3 and 10 [`per_benchmark_skill.csv`]: median skill of FA with greedy A is 0.83 / 0.84 (helm_mmlu), 0.73 / 0.83 (mteb_eng), 0.75 / 0.80 (mteb_multi);
  the lower tail is heavy on MTEB (16-17 % of benchmarks below 0.5 at k=10; a few benchmarks below 0, i.e. worse than the column mean).

## H4 theory check (Risk formula against measured held-out error) [`theory_gap.csv`, `theory_shape.csv`, `coherence.csv`]
* Median relative gap on the reducible part: synthetic true model 0.5 %; synthetic fitted 20 % (M=60 columns: 27 %) falling to 6.5 % at M=250 and 1.5 % at M=2000;
  parametric bootstrap from the fitted real models 16-24 %; real data 36 % (mteb_multi), 47 % (mteb_eng), 69 % (helm_mmlu). H4 (<= 15 % real, <= 10 % synthetic fitted)
  therefore **fails on real data** and on fitted synthetic data at small M; it holds at M >= 250 synthetic.
* The gap is mostly a roughly constant offset (about 0.08 in squared standardised units at k_max on the real tier): the k-dependent part is predicted much better (median relative
  gap 2-6 % bootstrap tier, 3-8 % real tier on the MTEB matrices, 23 % on helm_mmlu; correlation of predicted and measured curves above 0.997).
* Coherence, sampled submodularity ratio and the heuristic greedy bound are in `coherence.csv`; gamma-hat is an upper bound on the true ratio, so no rigorous
  lower-bound guarantee is claimed for the target-weighted objective.

## Matrix-Chernoff bound for random selection [`bound_check.csv`, `DERIVATIONS.md`]
* The sufficient k from the with-replacement source bound (eps 0.5, eta 0.1), with loadings fitted on all rows at the median fold rank of the main runs (r = 4 / 6 / 2), is
  459 (synthetic N=60, r=4), 506 (mteb_eng, N=70), 742 (mteb_multi, N=247) and 171 (helm_mmlu, N=57): vacuous at every k that can be run (the Chernoff value is 1.0 in all 35 rows).
  With the rank chosen on the full pool instead (5 / 6 / 1; sensitivity rows, `rank_source` column) the values are 551 / 742 / 57; only that helm_mmlu fit (r=1) is non-vacuous (8 rows).
  Without replacement the empirical violation share is never above the Chernoff value (35 of 35 rows), which is trivially true since that value is 1.0; the without-replacement
  extension is checked numerically only, not proved. The risk bound B(0.5) is below the measured mean reducible risk at k <= 3 on mteb_eng (equal at k=5) and k <= 8 on mteb_multi,
  above it from k=1 on helm_mmlu, and 2.7 (mteb_eng) / 1.6 (mteb_multi) / 3.2 (synthetic) times loose at k=30 (helm_mmlu 2.7 at k=20).
  The Jensen lower bound on random-selection risk holds in every row (35 of 35). The qualitative C5 prediction (random-minus-greedy gap shrinks with k and with coherence) rests on `coherence.csv`, not on the bound.

## Paired comparisons and bias check [`paired_comparisons.csv`, `bias_check.csv`]
* `paired_comparisons.py` (same test models, family-clustered paired bootstrap B=2000, seed 0; greedy A, factor model). Grouped minus random split at k=3 (RMSE difference, CI):
  mteb_eng +0.012 [-0.023, 0.058] (2.0 %); mteb_multi +0.012 [0.000, 0.023] (2.1 %); helm_mmlu +0.067 [0.003, 0.106] (14.2 %). Time-forward minus grouped on the *same* 56 / 53 test models:
  at k=3 mteb_eng +0.060 [-0.027, 0.129] (9.0 %), mteb_multi +0.112 [0.068, 0.156] (17.9 %); grid average +0.065 [0.020, 0.106] (10.2 %) and +0.089 [0.048, 0.130] (14.0 %).
  The unpaired time-forward RMSE (0.729 / 0.740) is on a different, later test set than grouped; the paired figures above are the penalty for the later-release split on the same models.
* Factor model minus other predictors, grid average, greedy A: ridge -0.000 / +0.006 / +0.013, LW +0.005 / +0.006 / +0.011, kNN +0.026 / +0.017 / -0.195, best simple +0.010 / +0.007 / -0.006
  (mteb_eng / mteb_multi / helm_mmlu; positive = factor model worse). On the MTEB pools the factor model is slightly worse than ridge/LW/best simple (CIs mostly exclude 0; helm_mmlu CIs include 0 except kNN).
* `bias_check.py`: signed error (predicted minus observed, original standardised units) at k=3, greedy A, grouped, by quintile of the model's score. Stratified by the full-row mean (includes the targets;
  as in `bias.csv`, reproduced to within 0.015) the strongest quintile is -0.103 / -0.089 / -0.028 (mteb_eng / mteb_multi / helm_mmlu). Data simulated from the factor model fitted to each pool
  (a correct model, 30 replicates) give -0.039 / -0.035 / -0.043 in the same quintile: roughly 40 % of the MTEB bias is the conditioning artefact (regression to the mean). Stratified by the mean of the observed
  S scores (which a correct model conditions on) the strongest quintile is still -0.058 [-0.150, 0.021] / -0.070 [-0.105, -0.028] / -0.003, so the MTEB bias is not entirely an artefact.
  Time-forward, strongest quintile: -0.284 (mteb_eng), -0.156 (mteb_multi).

