## Sparse leaderboard matrix (review M1) [`sparse_subsets.csv`, `sparse_curves.csv`, `sparse_refs.csv`, `sparse_gap.csv`, `sparse_closure.csv`, `sparse_kstar.csv`, `sparse_run_info.csv`]
Protocol (`bmk/sparse.py::run_sparse`, `run_sparse.py`; aggregation `aggregate_sparse.py`): the MTEB score matrix with its real missing cells (NaN in `results/data_out/sparse_*.npz`),
grouped (family-held-out) 10-fold over all models; training columns need >= 10 observed training cells and are standardised on their observed training cells; identity transform.
A model is tested if it has >= 30 observed cells in the subset. For each tested model and each of 3 random partitions: T = random round(0.3 |O|) of its observed cells (targets),
C = O \ T (candidates), O = its observed cells. Selectors pick k benchmarks from C (greedy A on the factor-model covariance restricted to C u T; random: 30 draws per k); the
rest of C is not used by the predictor (as in the dense protocol). Predictors: `colmean`; `fa` = masked-EM factor model (rank by inner 5-fold masked marginal likelihood, one-SE rule,
`fit_fa_masked`) conditioning on the selected cells; `pw` = pairwise-complete correlation matrix, PSD-clipped and shrunk toward I (shrink in {.02,...,.4} chosen on training rows);
`softimpute` = masked Soft-Impute fold-in (lambda chosen on training cells). `std_rmse` = sqrt of the mean over tested models of per-model MSE, on the standardised
training-column scale. `gap` = std_rmse(greedy A) - std_rmse(random), same predictor. LOBO analogue (`sparse_refs.csv`): predict T from all of C with `fa` and with `pw`; `lobo` is the lower
of the two (its source is in the `lobo_source` row), so closure C(k) = (colmean - err_k)/(colmean - lobo) can exceed 1 when a selected set beats the LOBO of the other predictor.
`kstar` = smallest k on the grid {1,2,3,4,5,6,8,10,12,15,20} with closure >= level, censored when never reached (CI codes a never-reached bootstrap replicate as 21).
Intervals: family-clustered bootstrap, B=2000, seed 0. 3 partitions x 30 random draws x 10 folds.

Subsets [`sparse_subsets.csv`]: `eng_full` = all MTEB(eng) models x tasks seen by >= 20 models (445 x 335, 21 % observed, median 60 observed cells per model, 337 models tested);
`eng_d30/d50/d70` = greedy trimming of English tasks and models to density 0.30 / 0.50 / 0.70 (332x295, 268x176, 226x134; 215-332 tested models, median 103-117 observed cells);
`all_d30/d50` = same on all MTEB tasks (331x705, 236x517; median 181 / 308 cells).
Because C holds at most 70 % of a model's observed cells (about 20-30 for the English subsets), the grid stops at k=20; k=30 is not available for most tested models.

* Greedy A beats random at every k on every subset, for `fa` and `pw` (gap CI excludes 0 in all cells except pw at k=20 on eng_full, CI [-0.105, 0.005]). Gap at k=3, `fa` (CI):
  eng_full -0.067 [-0.090,-0.045]; eng_d30 -0.067 [-0.094,-0.044]; eng_d50 -0.058 [-0.080,-0.047]; eng_d70 -0.047 [-0.074,-0.018]; all_d30 -0.156 [-0.205,-0.116]; all_d50 -0.335 [-0.629,-0.092] (-24 % of random).
  With 10 % of the random error as the pre-registered margin it is met at k=3 only on all_d30, all_d50 (and at k=1 on eng_d70 fa, -12 %); on the English subsets the gap at k=3 is 6-7 % of random.
* Closure at k=3, greedy A (`fa` / `pw`): eng_full 0.76 / 0.79; eng_d30 0.79 / 0.79; eng_d50 0.78 / 0.78; eng_d70 0.80 / 0.80; all_d30 0.75 / 0.78; all_d50 0.72 / 0.71 (CIs +-0.05-0.10, `sparse_closure.csv`).
  Random with `pw`: 0.66, 0.65, 0.68, 0.74, 0.59, 0.55; with `fa` 0.60, 0.63, 0.66, 0.73, 0.35, -0.08 (the last two with wide CIs).
* k*(0.9), greedy A `pw`: eng_full 6, eng_d30 8, eng_d50 12, eng_d70 8, all_d30 10, all_d50 20 (never reached before 20 with `fa` on eng_d50 and all_d50, all_d50 `pw` k*=20); random `pw`:
  20, censored (>20), 20, 15, censored, censored; random with `fa` is censored on every subset. CIs of k* are wide (e.g. eng_full pw greedy [3, >20]); the claim carried is the ordering greedy A < random, not a value of k*.
* Best predictor handling missing cells: `fa` is best or tied at k <= 5, `pw` slightly better at k >= 8 (e.g. eng_d70 k=20 std_rmse 0.544 fa vs 0.516 pw); both are far better than `colmean`
  (eng_d70: 1.086) and `softimpute` (0.82-1.04 at k=3-20, weak because the fold-in shrinks cells it cannot see). Greedy A + `fa` at k=3 is 0.65-1.05 vs colmean 1.09-1.37.
* Reading for the paper: the headline from this experiment is "greedy A-optimal selection beats random on the sparse matrix, closure about 0.7-0.8 at k=3 and about 0.9 by k=8-12 (pw) for densities 0.3-0.7, and the
  advantage does not vanish at density 0.3"; across k=1..20 it is 1.6-12 % of random RMSE on the English subsets (dense mteb_eng: 6 %), 4-24 % on the all-task subsets (dense mteb_multi: 11 %). It is **not** tested for density < 0.21 or for test models with < 30 observed cells.
* Conditions: identity transform only (no logit selection on sparse runs); different task sets from the dense pools, so RMSE levels are not comparable to the dense results; std_rmse levels (1.0-1.4 for colmean) are
  higher than in the dense runs because test models are tested on their own random observed cells and column means ignore each model's overall level; the LOBO reference is itself noisy (CI +-0.4), so closure is
  noisier than the gap.

## Small panels and the union matrix [`small_panels*.csv`, `union_overlap.csv`] (descriptive only, no k*, no H1/H2/H4 verdict)
* HELM Lite (10 scenarios), HELM Capabilities (5) and a 25-model x 16-benchmark union of HELM Lite and Open LLM Leaderboard (12 families) are case studies with N < 12;
  the name-matching rule and the exclusion of ambiguous names are in `union_overlap.py`. Overlaps: 25 models in HELM Lite and Open LLM Leaderboard, 23 with LiveBench,
  72 models in at least two sources; the complete-case union used is the 25 x 16 block above.

## Conditions to carry into the text
* Every statement is about standardised RMSE on held-out families; "closure" is relative to the LOBO reference, not to the noise floor.
* The in-sample optimistic reference and the oracle selector are test-leaking and appear only as labelled bounds.
* MTEB matrices are dense pools chosen by us (see `datasets.csv`); HELM MMLU has a binomial sampling floor [`helm_mmlu_binomial_floor.csv`].
